{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310812,"nonce":"5VsY6ytQ/e3kVIh6jYNz+M53iQM=","title":"SHIPPED 64993625: stdlib cache entries drop the program hash when the .bsym manifest is loaded — a second program in a project goes 222ms ...","message":"**SHIPPED `64993625` (2026-08-17).** `hash_source_for_config` no longer folds\n`user_program_hash()` for a stdlib source WHEN `stdlib_manifest_loaded`. A\nsecond, different program in a project: `cache_hit=42 cache_miss=0`,\n**222ms -\u003e 22ms**.\n\n**THE CONDITION IS THE MANIFEST, NOT THE MODULE.** Two earlier attempts keyed\non properties of the module and both were wrong — dropping the fold outright\n(`7997eedd`, reverted `e4ec2b11`) and dropping it for modules declaring no\ngeneric ([[bugs-nongeneric-stdlib-modules-still-program-dependent]]). The\ndiscriminating experiment is a 2x2:\n\n| | sharing OFF | sharing ON |\n|---|---|---|\n| lazy stdlib (`CompilationConfig::fast()`, skips the manifest) | pass | **stack overflow** |\n| eager (`Default`/`strict`, loads it — what the CLI uses) | pass | **pass** |\n\nRestoring a module is equivalent to compiling it only when the declarations it\nresolves against exist, and those come from the `.bsym` manifest. `lazy_stdlib`\nskips it (compilation.rs ~935), so a restored module resolves against nothing.\n\n**THE ARTIFACTS WERE NEVER PROGRAM-DEPENDENT** — measured, contradicting the\nold comment at the fold: 42/42 identical for two ordinary programs, 46/46 for\ntwo that instantiate `Channel\u003cT\u003e` differently.\n**TIMESTAMP TRAP:** normalise the two 5-byte LEB128 varints after the source\nhash (`source_timestamp`/`compile_timestamp`, both set to `now`) or sequential\nruns look like 12 differing modules.\n\n**`test_sys_thread`'s 2 failures are an ASSERTION ARTIFACT, not a canary.**\nThey fire identically with eager stdlib and NO sharing, because the assertion\nwants `sys_thread_join` in the module's own function list and any path that\nrestores declarations instead of compiling them puts it elsewhere. The\nprograms are correct standalone either way (verified verbatim). `e4ec2b11`\nrecords these same assertions flip-flopping before. **Check behaviour\nstandalone before treating them as evidence.**\n\n**MASKING TO KNOW ABOUT:** the suite now reads 184/184 (was 183/184) because\n`test_compress` passes once another program has warmed the cache. Its real bug\nis still there — alone in a fresh project it fails `Cannot find type\n'haxe.io'`, at HEAD, with or without this change. The suite no longer shows it.\n\n**THE FIRST PROGRAM IS FIXED TOO — no code change, the snapshot just had to be\nregenerated.** Its entries were written with the OLD key (program folded in),\nso they could never match. After `build -\u003e cargo run --release -p snapshot-gen\n-\u003e build`, a first program in a FRESH project gets `cache_hit=42 cache_miss=0`\nand **cold wall time goes 0.29s -\u003e 0.07s** (repo-root context, base vs HEAD,\n3 runs each, steady). Both hash AND build id match — verified with\n`RAYZOR_DEBUG_BLADE=1`.\n\n**MEASUREMENT TRAP THAT NEARLY MISLED ME:** cold numbers depend on the WORKING\nDIRECTORY. In a bare scratch dir with no project config the old binary\ncollects `imports=0` and compiles in 38ms; in the repo root the same binary\ncollects 42 imports and takes 290ms. Compare binaries only within the SAME\ndirectory, and say which one.\n\n**STILL OPEN:** nothing on this thread. `test_compress` remains broken alone\nin a fresh project (`Cannot find type 'haxe.io'`) — pre-existing, now masked\nin the suite by cache sharing.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_stdlib_cache_shares_when_manifest_loaded` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}